      "source": "Bihar Google Alert",
      "title": "Bihar's Parasi school mid-day meal scare: Students ask for salt, cook mistakenly gives detergent",
      "url": "https://www.cnbctv18.com/india/bihar-school-mid-day-meal-scare-students-ask-for-salt-cook-mistakenly-gives-them-detergent-34-hospitalised-19972446.htm",
      "date": "Wed, 19 Aug 2026 05:03:20 IST",
      "content": "Bihar's Parasi school mid-day meal scare: Students ask for salt, cook mistakenly gives detergent; 34 hospitalised 34 students fell ill after a mid-day meal at Middle School, Parasi, Nalanda, Bihar. Detergent powder was allegedly mistaken for salt. All students are stable. By CNBCTV18.com August 19, 2026, 10:32:07 AM IST (Updated) 2 Min Read A routine school lunch turned into a health scare in Bihar’s Nalanda district after 34 students reportedly fell ill following a mid-day meal at a government school. The incident took place at Middle School, Parasi, in the Noorsarai police station area, on Tuesday. Seventeen boys and 17 girls from the school became sick. The children were taken to Biharsharif Sadar Hospital for treatment after developing symptoms. District officials later said all of them were stable and out of danger, according to PTI. Detergent powder allegedly mistaken for salt According to the school’s headmaster, Shailendra Kumar Singh, the meal was supplied through an NGO and included rice and soybean-potato curry, reported The Indian Express. The headmaster said the school served the meal in multiple sittings. It reportedly happened after a cook accidentally added detergent powder to a student’s meal when extra salt was requested. “During the second sitting, a student, identified as the granddaughter of a school cook, reportedly asked for additional salt. The cook allegedly picked up detergent powder instead of salt by mistake and added it to the girl’s food,” he was quoted as saying by The Indian Express. Other students also asked for extra salt and were allegedly served the same detergent powder. Soon afterwards, some children complained of throat and stomach pain. 34 children taken to hospital The school initially provided warm salt water before the affected students were shifted to hospital for further treatment. District officials said all 34 children were examined by doctors and were out of danger. The meal had reportedly been served to 132 students, while two cooks and two teachers had also eaten the food before it was served to the children. Officials said samples of the meal would be examined to determine what caused the illness. Officials order inquiry Bihar Rural Development Minister Shravan Kumar visited the hospital and met the affected students, while officials from the education department and local administration also took stock of the situation. Kumar said an inquiry would be conducted to establish how the lapse occurred during the preparation and distribution of the meal. The district administration also said no adverse health effects were detected among the children at the time of assessment. No FIR or complaint had been filed in connection with the incident at the time of reporting. Continue Reading (Edited by : Sudarsanan Mani ) First Published: Aug 19, 2026 10:27 AM IST Tags Bihar Food government schools health care",
      "content_chars": 2898,
      "content_words": 460,
      "type": "State News"
    },
